WEBVTT

NOTE week11-clip3-conditioning
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:05.026 line:2 align:center
Unconditional sampling gives you a valid
structure, but not the one you need.

2
00:00:05.026 --> 00:00:08.521 line:2 align:center
A real design project asks
for something specific:

3
00:00:08.521 --> 00:00:13.883 line:2 align:center
hold this functional motif in
place, or bind this target protein.

4
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:16.601 line:2 align:center
Conditioning is the direct answer.

5
00:00:16.601 --> 00:00:21.826 line:2 align:center
Give the denoiser the motif's coordinates
and hold them fixed at every step.

6
00:00:21.826 --> 00:00:25.213 line:2 align:center
The motif never moves, and
the scaffold is sampled,

7
00:00:25.213 --> 00:00:29.083 line:2 align:center
so one motif gives you
many different scaffolds.

8
00:00:29.283 --> 00:00:34.066 line:2 align:center
Guidance is a different lever,
borrowed from image diffusion.

9
00:00:34.066 --> 00:00:38.261 line:2 align:center
Mix the conditional and
unconditional predictions:

10
00:00:38.261 --> 00:00:43.547 line:2 align:center
0.2, plus 3 times 0.5 minus 0.2,

11
00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:49.422 line:2 align:center
gives 1.1. A larger w follows the
condition harder and cuts diversity.

12
00:00:49.422 --> 00:00:55.417 line:2 align:center
RFdiffusion does not do this; it
conditions the denoiser directly.

13
00:00:55.617 --> 00:01:01.256 line:2 align:center
On 25 motif-scaffolding
benchmarks, RFdiffusion solved 23,

14
00:01:01.256 --> 00:01:06.605 line:2 align:center
against 19 for RFjoint inpainting
and 15 for hallucination,

15
00:01:06.605 --> 00:01:10.316 line:2 align:center
using one set of settings
for every problem.

16
00:01:10.316 --> 00:01:13.883 line:2 align:center
Note that this success
is scored in silico.

17
00:01:14.083 --> 00:01:17.793 line:2 align:center
Designed binders were
tested against five targets,

18
00:01:17.793 --> 00:01:23.707 line:2 align:center
among them influenza hemagglutinin
and PD-L1. 19% worked experimentally,

19
00:01:23.707 --> 00:01:27.334 line:2 align:center
from fewer than a hundred
designs per target.

20
00:01:27.334 --> 00:01:30.929 line:2 align:center
The best flu binder reached 28 nanomolar,

21
00:01:30.929 --> 00:01:36.550 line:2 align:center
and a cryo-EM map matched
the design to 0.63 angstroms.

22
00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:39.400 line:2 align:center
Read the in silico numbers with care.

23
00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:43.542 line:2 align:center
The designs are scored by
AlphaFold family predictors trained

24
00:01:43.542 --> 00:01:48.982 line:2 align:center
on the same structures, so the designer
and the judge can agree and both be wrong.

25
00:01:48.982 --> 00:01:55.713 line:2 align:center
The 19% is the number that counts,
because it came from a wet lab.
